Subject: libEGL: initialize a display whose lost GPU device gives no extension string

eglInitialize on a display whose GPU device is lost can succeed while the driver's eglQueryString
returns NULL for EGL_EXTENSIONS. egl_cache_t::initialize took the length of that string and
crashed with a null pointer in strlen. In an app, hwui's retry after a GPU device loss
(EglManager::deviceAnswers) runs eglInitialize every few hundred milliseconds until the host GPU
answers, so a renderer that was gone for a moment ended the app's render thread, and with it the
app. The cache now skips the blob cache setup when there is no extension string, and the display
is initialized again on the next eglInitialize (0003), so hwui's probe finds no vendor string and
tries again later.

Applies to frameworks/native at ae266dcb706d083868578cfedce381ef44488a07 (Android 17).
---
diff --git a/opengl/libs/EGL/egl_cache.cpp b/opengl/libs/EGL/egl_cache.cpp
index 59745c0..f79e3b4 100644
--- a/opengl/libs/EGL/egl_cache.cpp
+++ b/opengl/libs/EGL/egl_cache.cpp
@@ -87,6 +87,13 @@ void egl_cache_t::initialize(egl_display_t* display) {
     egl_connection_t* const cnx = &gEGLImpl;
     if (display && cnx->dso && cnx->major >= 0 && cnx->minor >= 0) {
         const char* exts = display->disp.queryString.extensions;
+        if (exts == nullptr) {
+            // The driver initialized the display but gives no extension string: its GPU device
+            // is lost. There is no blob cache to set up on it, and the display is initialized
+            // again once the device is back.
+            ALOGW("eglInitialize: the driver gave no extension string; the GPU device is lost");
+            return;
+        }
         size_t bcExtLen = strlen(BC_EXT_STR);
         size_t extsLen = strlen(exts);
         bool equal = !strcmp(BC_EXT_STR, exts);
